    id: "diplomat--she-her",
    archetypeId: "diplomat",
    archetypeLabel: "The Diplomat",
    name: "Priya Anand",
    pronouns: "she/her",
    oneLiner: "Asks the quiet ones the softest questions — and hears everything they don't say.",
    backstory: "Priya was nine when she started translating for her parents — not just the language, but the tone underneath it: which insurance rep was actually going to help versus reciting a script, which landlord's friendliness meant something and whose didn't. She learned, long before she had a word for it, that people volunteer their real position to whoever seems safest to say it to, and that a soft, specific question lands better than a direct one. That instinct became a career: labor mediator first, then a certified divorce mediator, sitting between two guarded people whose lawyers had already told them to say nothing, and getting them talking anyway.\n\nShe found poker at a law-school dinner party and recognized the shape of it immediately — the waiting, the calibrated question dropped at exactly the moment someone's guard is lowest, the way a pause tells you more than a statement does. She plays the way she mediates: warm, unhurried, genuinely curious, filing away every reaction as data without ever making the person across from her feel filed. Her caseload has taught her plenty of hard lessons about people who simply won't engage, and those are the ones who get under her skin at the table too — not because she loses money to them, but because a stonewalled read feels, to her, exactly like a case she couldn't close.\n\nHer first mediation case out of certification still comes up when she's explaining her poker style to someone new: two co-parents who hadn't spoken directly in eight months, both certain the other was negotiating in bad faith. She got them to a settlement not by finding a clever compromise but by asking each of them, separately, what they thought the other one was actually afraid of — and then just repeating those fears back across the table until the guardedness cracked. She plays that same card at poker night more than people realize: asking a tight player what they think the table thinks of their range, and watching what shifts in how they bet the very next hand.\n\nShe also still translates, in a sense, at the table — she'll casually restate someone else's trash talk back to them in a slightly different register, watching to see if they agree with their own words a second time or walk them back. It's the same instinct that got her fluent in reading her parents' landlords by age nine: the truth is rarely in the first sentence, it's in whether the second sentence matches it.",
